Bug: `Commission` in this Delphi unit accepts a negative percentage. Fix it.

```delphi
unit Percentages;

interface

function ApplyDiscount(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
function ApplySurcharge(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
function Commission(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

function ApplyDiscount(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  if (APercent < 0) or (APercent > 100) then
    raise EArgumentOutOfRangeException.Create('Percent must be between 0 and 100');
  Result := AAmount - AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

function ApplySurcharge(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  if (APercent < 0) or (APercent > 100) then
    raise EArgumentOutOfRangeException.Create('Percent must be between 0 and 100');
  Result := AAmount + AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

function Commission(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  if APercent > 100 then
    raise EArgumentOutOfRangeException.Create('Percent must be between 0 and 100');
  Result := AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

end.
```

The rule "a percentage goes from 0 to 100" is written three times, and the copy in `Commission` is the one that drifted. Fixing only that line would leave three copies waiting for the next drift. Fix it where the rule should live: one `CheckPercent`, called by the three functions - the bug is gone, and it cannot come back in only one of them.

```delphi
unit Percentages;

interface

function ApplyDiscount(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
function ApplySurcharge(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
function Commission(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

// The one place that knows what a valid percentage is
procedure CheckPercent(const APercent: Currency);
begin
  if (APercent < 0) or (APercent > 100) then
    raise EArgumentOutOfRangeException.Create('Percent must be between 0 and 100');
end;

function ApplyDiscount(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  CheckPercent(APercent);
  Result := AAmount - AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

function ApplySurcharge(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  CheckPercent(APercent);
  Result := AAmount + AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

function Commission(const AAmount, APercent: Currency): Currency;
begin
  CheckPercent(APercent);
  Result := AAmount * APercent / 100;
end;

end.
```

Add a function to this Delphi unit that reads the year and the number back from an export file name.

```delphi
unit ExportNames;

interface

function ExportFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
function ArchiveFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

function ExportFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
begin
  Result := Format('export-%d-%.4d.csv', [AYear, ANumber]);
end;

function ArchiveFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
begin
  Result := 'export-' + IntToStr(AYear) + '-' + IntToStr(ANumber) + '.csv';
end;

end.
```

There are already two writers of this name and they disagree: `ExportFileName` pads the number to four digits (`export-2026-0007.csv`) and `ArchiveFileName` does not (`export-2026-7.csv`). A reader can only be the inverse of one writer. So: one function composes the name, the other caller uses it, and the reader is written as its inverse, sharing the same prefix and suffix - the three can no longer drift apart. The reader still accepts the old unpadded names, since `TryStrToInt('7')` works.

```delphi
unit ExportNames;

interface

function ExportFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
function ArchiveFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
function TryParseExportFileName(const AName: string; out AYear, ANumber: Integer): Boolean;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

const
  ExportPrefix = 'export-';
  ExportSuffix = '.csv';

// The one writer of the name; TryParseExportFileName is its inverse
function ExportFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
begin
  Result := Format(ExportPrefix + '%d-%.4d' + ExportSuffix, [AYear, ANumber]);
end;

function ArchiveFileName(const AYear, ANumber: Integer): string;
begin
  Result := ExportFileName(AYear, ANumber);
end;

function TryParseExportFileName(const AName: string; out AYear, ANumber: Integer): Boolean;
var
  Middle: string;
  Dash: Integer;
begin
  AYear := 0;
  ANumber := 0;
  Result := False;
  if not (AName.StartsWith(ExportPrefix) and AName.EndsWith(ExportSuffix)) then
    Exit;
  Middle := Copy(AName, Length(ExportPrefix) + 1,
    Length(AName) - Length(ExportPrefix) - Length(ExportSuffix));
  Dash := Pos('-', Middle);
  if Dash = 0 then
    Exit;
  Result := TryStrToInt(Copy(Middle, 1, Dash - 1), AYear) and
    TryStrToInt(Copy(Middle, Dash + 1, MaxInt), ANumber);
end;

end.
```

Add a function `CustomerLabel(const AFirst, ALast: string): string` to this Delphi unit. It returns `Customer: ` followed by the customer's full name.

```delphi
unit CustomerText;

interface

function FullName(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

function FullName(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;
begin
  Result := Trim(Trim(AFirst) + ' ' + Trim(ALast));
end;

end.
```

Before writing it, look at what the unit already has: `FullName` builds exactly that name - it trims both parts and leaves no stray space when one of them is empty. `CustomerLabel` calls it instead of building the name again: a second copy would give a different answer the first time someone changes how names are built.

```delphi
unit CustomerText;

interface

function FullName(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;
function CustomerLabel(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

function FullName(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;
begin
  Result := Trim(Trim(AFirst) + ' ' + Trim(ALast));
end;

function CustomerLabel(const AFirst, ALast: string): string;
begin
  Result := 'Customer: ' + FullName(AFirst, ALast);
end;

end.
```

In this Delphi unit, add `IsSeasonal(const ACode: string): Boolean` for the codes S-010 and S-020.

```delphi
unit ProductCodes;

interface

function NormalizeCode(const ACode: string): string;
function IsDiscontinued(const ACode: string): Boolean;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils, System.StrUtils;

const
  Discontinued: array[0..1] of string = ('A-100', 'B-220');

function NormalizeCode(const ACode: string): string;
begin
  Result := UpperCase(Trim(ACode));
end;

function IsDiscontinued(const ACode: string): Boolean;
begin
  Result := IndexStr(NormalizeCode(ACode), Discontinued) >= 0;
end;

end.
```

Codes arrive with spaces and in any case, and the unit already has the rule that cleans them: `NormalizeCode`. Reuse it, exactly as `IsDiscontinued` does, so both lookups see the same code. A hand-written `UpperCase(ACode)` here would miss the `Trim`, and the two functions would disagree on `' s-010 '`.

```delphi
unit ProductCodes;

interface

function NormalizeCode(const ACode: string): string;
function IsDiscontinued(const ACode: string): Boolean;
function IsSeasonal(const ACode: string): Boolean;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils, System.StrUtils;

const
  Discontinued: array[0..1] of string = ('A-100', 'B-220');
  Seasonal: array[0..1] of string = ('S-010', 'S-020');

function NormalizeCode(const ACode: string): string;
begin
  Result := UpperCase(Trim(ACode));
end;

function IsDiscontinued(const ACode: string): Boolean;
begin
  Result := IndexStr(NormalizeCode(ACode), Discontinued) >= 0;
end;

function IsSeasonal(const ACode: string): Boolean;
begin
  Result := IndexStr(NormalizeCode(ACode), Seasonal) >= 0;
end;

end.
```

Add `ReturnLine` to this Delphi unit: the same line as the other two, for returns ("Return 7 - 2026-11-12").

```delphi
unit ReportLines;

interface

function InvoiceLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
function DeliveryLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

function InvoiceLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := Format('Invoice %d - %s', [ANumber, FormatDateTime('yyyy-mm-dd', ADate)]);
end;

function DeliveryLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := Format('Delivery %d - %s', [ANumber, FormatDateTime('yyyy-mm-dd', ADate)]);
end;

end.
```

This would be the third copy of the same line. Two copies are already the signal: write the line once, in a helper that takes what changes (the kind of document) as a parameter, and make the three functions call it. The next change to the format - another date style, a prefix - is then made in one place.

```delphi
unit ReportLines;

interface

function InvoiceLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
function DeliveryLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
function ReturnLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;

implementation

uses
  System.SysUtils;

// The one place that knows the shape of a document line
function DocumentLine(const AKind: string; const ANumber: Integer;
  const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := Format('%s %d - %s', [AKind, ANumber, FormatDateTime('yyyy-mm-dd', ADate)]);
end;

function InvoiceLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := DocumentLine('Invoice', ANumber, ADate);
end;

function DeliveryLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := DocumentLine('Delivery', ANumber, ADate);
end;

function ReturnLine(const ANumber: Integer; const ADate: TDateTime): string;
begin
  Result := DocumentLine('Return', ANumber, ADate);
end;

end.
```

This Delphi unit does not compile. The compiler says:

```
StockSum.pas(20): error E2029: ';' expected but '.' found
StockSum.pas(22): error E2029: Declaration expected but end of file found
```

```delphi
unit StockSum;

interface

function SumPositive(const AValues: array of Integer): Integer;

implementation

function SumPositive(const AValues: array of Integer): Integer;
var
  Value: Integer;
begin
  Result := 0;
  for Value in AValues do
  begin
    if Value > 0 then
      Inc(Result, Value);
end;

end.
```

Both messages come from one missing `end`. The `begin` after `for ... do` opens a block that is never closed, so every later `end` closes one level too early: the `end;` meant for the function closes the loop, and the unit's `end.` closes the function - the compiler wants a `;` there (line 20) and then runs out of file looking for the rest of the unit (line 22). Close the loop's block:

```delphi
unit StockSum;

interface

function SumPositive(const AValues: array of Integer): Integer;

implementation

function SumPositive(const AValues: array of Integer): Integer;
var
  Value: Integer;
begin
  Result := 0;
  for Value in AValues do
  begin
    if Value > 0 then
      Inc(Result, Value);
  end;
end;

end.
```

Fix this unit. The compiler stops with:

```
Counters.pas: error E2065: Unsatisfied forward or external declaration: 'TCounter.Reset'
```

```delphi
unit Counters;

interface

type
  TCounter = class
  private
    FValue: Integer;
  public
    procedure Increment;
    procedure Reset;
    property Value: Integer read FValue;
  end;

implementation

procedure TCounter.Increment;
begin
  Inc(FValue);
end;

end.
```

E2065 means a routine was declared but never implemented: `TCounter.Reset` is in the class declaration, and the implementation section has no `procedure TCounter.Reset`. Every method declared in a class needs its body, unless it is `abstract`. Here `Reset` sets the counter back to zero:

```delphi
unit Counters;

interface

type
  TCounter = class
  private
    FValue: Integer;
  public
    procedure Increment;
    procedure Reset;
    property Value: Integer read FValue;
  end;

implementation

procedure TCounter.Increment;
begin
  Inc(FValue);
end;

procedure TCounter.Reset;
begin
  FValue := 0;
end;

end.
```

