Delphi string literals have no escape sequences: inside the quotes, `'#$2714'` is six ordinary characters. A character code goes **outside** the quotes, concatenated to the literal: `'Sent ' + #$2714`. The source stays plain ASCII and the string holds the real character.

```delphi
unit SendStatus;

interface

function SentText: string;

implementation

function SentText: string;
begin
  // U+2714 HEAVY CHECK MARK, written outside the quotes
  Result := 'Sent ' + #$2714;
end;

end.
```

Add a global function `IsWeekend(const ADate: TDateTime): Boolean` to this Delphi unit. It must be a plain function that other units can call, not a method of TShift.

```delphi
unit Shifts;

interface

type
  TShift = class
  private
    FStart: TDateTime;
  public
    constructor Create(const AStart: TDateTime);
    property Start: TDateTime read FStart;
  end;

implementation

constructor TShift.Create(const AStart: TDateTime);
begin
  inherited Create;
  FStart := AStart;
end;

end.
```

A global function is declared in the `interface` section, outside the class, and implemented in `implementation` without a class prefix. `DayOfTheWeek` (System.DateUtils) is ISO 8601: Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7, so the weekend is 6 and 7. (`DayOfWeek` in System.SysUtils counts from Sunday = 1, a classic source of off-by-one bugs.) Only the implementation needs System.DateUtils, so it goes in the implementation `uses` clause.

```delphi
unit Shifts;

interface

type
  TShift = class
  private
    FStart: TDateTime;
  public
    constructor Create(const AStart: TDateTime);
    property Start: TDateTime read FStart;
  end;

function IsWeekend(const ADate: TDateTime): Boolean;

implementation

uses
  System.DateUtils;

constructor TShift.Create(const AStart: TDateTime);
begin
  inherited Create;
  FStart := AStart;
end;

function IsWeekend(const ADate: TDateTime): Boolean;
begin
  // DayOfTheWeek is ISO 8601: Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7
  Result := DayOfTheWeek(ADate) >= 6;
end;

end.
```

